<section id="data-view" class="view" hidden>
  <header class="page-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 / SOURCES & ASSUMPTIONS</p><h1>Behind the ledger.</h1><p class="subtitle">A database of transactions and metadata, with a record of where each import came from.</p></div></header>
  <div class="detail-grid"><article class="card"><h2>Your ledger database</h2><div id="data-details"></div><button id="reload-button" class="outline-button">↺ Refresh ledger</button></article><article class="card"><h2>Import data</h2><p class="input-note">Bank CSV imports add transactions, account names, categories and sender/receiver metadata. Canonical JSON imports can add or update these independently. Reimporting the same source keeps existing transactions and corrections.</p><label>Import format<select id="ingest-adapter"><option value="auto">Detect from file extension</option><option value="norwegian-bank-csv">Norwegian bank CSV</option><option value="canonical-v1">Canonical JSON (transactions + metadata)</option></select></label><button id="data-import-button" class="outline-button">↑ Select import file</button><p id="ingest-status" class="input-note" aria-live="polite"></p><div id="ingest-history"></div></article></div>
  <article class="card"><div class="card-heading"><h2>Export your complete ledger</h2><span class="badge">All saved data</span></div><p>Transactions, accounts, every dated balance, categories, counterparties, corrections, forecast settings and import history.</p><div class="export-actions"><button id="export-ledger-json" class="outline-button">↓ JSON archive</button><button id="export-ledger-sqlite" class="outline-button">↓ SQLite backup</button></div><p class="input-note">Choose JSON for a readable archive or your own tools. Choose SQLite for a complete database restore. Pending saves finish before the download starts.</p><p class="input-note">These private files include account identifiers and original import rows. Sign-in credentials and this browser’s appearance preferences are excluded.</p><p id="ledger-export-status" class="input-note" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p></article>
  <article class="card"><div class="card-heading"><h2>Accounts in this ledger</h2><button class="text-button" data-view="accounts">Manage balances →</button></div><div id="account-details"></div><p class="input-note">Account identifiers are matched inside the private database. Payment descriptions remain as supplied by the source.</p></article>
  <article class="card method-card"><h2>What the numbers mean</h2><dl><dt>Money in</dt><dd>All positive amounts except transfers between named accounts. It can include salary, refunds, loan proceeds, and incoming transfers. Forecast regular income uses salary rows only.</dd><dt>Spending</dt><dd>Negative amounts except internal transfers and rows classified as savings contributions. Loan repayments remain cash outflows; this view does not separate principal from interest or calculate net worth.</dd><dt>Savings & investments</dt><dd>Rows tagged “Sparing” and “Mikrospar til …”, and automatic transfers from available cash into owned reserves or investments, are shown separately. They reduce available cash, but may still be your money in an account outside this export. Track their values separately in Accounts, even without an export.</dd><dt>Internal transfers</dt><dd>The source identifies both accounts as yours, or you link the payment to an owned account in its review dialog. Automatic movements between available accounts do not count as income or spending. Transfers from available cash into reserves or investments count as savings outflows. Withdrawals into available cash are nonrecurring money in. Explicit classifications take priority. Dated account values are separate observations and never create payments.</dd><dt>Period statistics</dt><dd>Daily spending divides spending by calendar days within the first and latest recorded dates, including days without recorded payments. The typical payment is the median spending amount: the middle value, or the mean of the two middle values, rounded to the nearest øre. One-off payments remain in historical statistics. Savings contribution rate divides savings contributions by all incoming money, excluding internal transfers.</dd><dt>Period comparisons</dt><dd>Completed months compare with the preceding set of calendar months. Custom dates compare with the same number of preceding days. Month-to-date compares with the same dates in the previous month when those dates exist. Both full date ranges must fall within recorded history. Missing months inside that history count as zero recorded activity; an import does not prove that every payment is present.</dd><dt>Payment activity</dt><dd>Account and recipient averages follow the selected account confirmation and category across all saved dates. They use nonzero payments in each direction. Matching debit and credit entries of an internal transfer count as one payment. Search and direction only filter the payment history list.</dd><dt>Charts over time</dt><dd>Overview and filtered transaction charts use daily, 7-day, or monthly totals. Automatic chooses daily for up to 45 days, 7-day for up to 180 days, and monthly for longer ranges. Seven-day buckets start at the selected covered start date. First and last buckets can be partial. Empty buckets inside recorded history mean no recorded activity. Cash change accumulates from zero and is not an account balance. Small card charts show the stated totals over time; they do not predict future movement. Forecast compares your scenario with multiple selected projection methods over 1–60 months. History shown independently adds up to 24 months of cash reconstructed from recorded movements and the starting balance. These are not observed bank balances. Historical methods use complete months before the earlier of the balance month and latest transaction month; seasonal repeat requires 12 months. Hover or tap a plot to inspect exact amounts and full dates. With a chart focused, use Left and Right Arrow, Home, or End to select a point; Escape dismisses the tooltip. Open “View chart figures” for the full table.</dd><dt>Repeated rows</dt><dd>Identical rows are kept. Multiple identical purchases and micro-saving payments can be real. The export provides no unique bank transaction ID, so duplicates cannot be removed reliably.</dd><dt>Currency</dt><dd>All charts use “Beløp”, the bank's booked NOK amount. Original foreign-currency amounts are not added together.</dd><dt>Private storage</dt><dd>Transactions, dated account balances, imports, forecast settings and edits are stored in the private database. Theme preferences stay in this browser. The source repository and container images contain no financial data. No external fonts, analytics, or AI calls are used.</dd></dl><button id="clear-saved" class="outline-button danger">Reset forecast settings & transaction edits</button></article>
</section>
